{
  "comment": "The objective is Bayesian optimization of an antimicrobial peptide sequence to maximize efficacy against APEX bacteria_0. The metric to minimize is the MIC (Minimum Inhibitory Concentration) for the positive class (bacteria), effectively maximizing the negative mean MIC. We have 100 initial measurements from a reference sequence (RRIYTIWEHNTKNLLRLKSIFS). The search space is constrained to canonical amino acids (ACDEFGHIKLMNPQRSTVWY), length 1-30, at most one Cysteine (C), and a strict similarity constraint (Levenshtein distance <= 25% of reference length) to ensure chemical tractability and avoid random walks. The optimization strategy must balance exploration of the similarity-constrained neighborhood with exploitation of the current best-performing regions in the MIC landscape.",
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "name": "Local Optimization via Hydrophobic Core Enhancement",
      "rationale": "The reference sequence RRIYTIWEHNTKNLLRLKSIFS contains a central hydrophobic stretch (RYTIWEH...LLRL). Antimicrobial activity often correlates with the amphipathic nature and hydrophobic moment. Mutating polar or charged residues in the core to hydrophobic ones (like L, I, V, F, W) while maintaining the overall amphipathic helix/turn structure could enhance membrane insertion and bacterial killing, thereby lowering MIC. This hypothesis focuses on the 'LLRL' and surrounding regions.",
      "confidence": "High",
      "points": [
        {
          "sequence": "RRIYTIWEHNTKNLLRIKSIFS"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "N-terminal Charge Modulation for Selectivity",
      "rationale": "The N-terminus (RRIY) is rich in positive charges, crucial for interacting with negatively charged bacterial membranes. However, excessive charge can reduce permeability or increase hemolysis. While the constraint limits drastic changes, swapping the Arginines (R) for other basic residues like Lysine (K) or Histidine (H) might fine-tune the pKa and charge distribution at physiological pH, potentially improving selectivity or uptake without violating the 0.75 similarity threshold. This explores the boundary of the charge-hydrophobicity trade-off.",
      "confidence": "Medium",
      "points": [
        {
          "sequence": "KKIYTIWEHNTKNLLRLKSIFS"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "C-terminal Flexibility and Anchor Introduction",
      "rationale": "The C-terminal region (KSIFS) is flexible. Introducing a Proline (P) or Glycine (G) here could alter the peptide's conformational entropy, potentially stabilizing an active conformation that exposes more hydrophobic residues to the membrane interface. Additionally, ensuring no Cysteine is present (as per the 'at most one' rule, and none in reference) allows for a cleaner hydrophobic surface. A mutation to a more rigid or anchoring residue like Isoleucine (I) or Valine (V) in the C-terminal tail might enhance binding affinity.",
      "confidence": "Medium",
      "points": [
        {
          "sequence": "RRIYTIWEHNTKNLLRLKPIFS"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

