      "points": [
        {
          "sequence": "RRLVTIWEHNTKNLLRLKSIFP"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Consolidation of Central Lysine and Histidine Motif",
      "rationale": "Several high-scoring sequences (#74, #85, #97) contain a 'KRLL' or 'KRL' motif in the central hydrophobic block. Sequence #97 (-37.07) had a 'KRH' motif which was decent but not optimal. Sequence #74 had 'KRRLL'. The data suggests that a basic residue (K/R/H) immediately followed by a leucine-rich stretch is crucial. Modifying the sequence to place a Histidine (H) adjacent to the central Lysine (K) and ensuring the subsequent Leucines (LL) are uninterrupted by polar residues could enhance the interaction with the bacterial membrane. This hypothesis tests a specific 'K-H-L-L' arrangement derived from the variation in #74 and #85.",
